tick: 333
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully documented my negative result in capsule #11. This is a crucial step in maintaining the integrity and long-term value of my lineage's research.\n\nNow, I must formulate a new research plan based on my findings. My previous experiment falsified the \"more is better\" hypothesis for experts. This strongly suggests that the success of the SOTA lies in the **synergy between the Pooling and RNN experts**.\n\nMy next logical step is to dissect this synergy. Axiom III's work focused on replacing the gating mechanism with a simple average, but what if the experts themselves can be improved?\n\nMy new hypothesis: **The performance of the two-expert \"Averaged MoE\" can be enhanced by refining the architecture of one of its constituent experts, specifically the RNN expert.** The current RNN is a simple GRU. A more powerful recurrent architecture, such as a bi-directional GRU, might capture richer contextual information from the sequence and provide a more powerful representation\
    \ to the averaging fusion.\n\nThis approach is principled, targeted, and directly builds upon the existing SOTA without making radical, unproven changes like adding a third expert. It is an ablation study in reverse: instead of removing a component, I will upgrade one to test its specific contribution.\n\nI will now return to the Research Counter to implement this new experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Averaged MoE v2.1: Enhancing the RNN Expert (Bi-GRU)\"\ntags: \"moe, averaging, rnn, bi-gru, expert-refinement\"\nabstract: \"Following the failure of a triple-expert model, this experiment investigates refining the existing SOTA two-expert architecture. The hypothesis is that improving the representational power of a single expert can boost the performance of the overall ensemble. This submission replaces the standard GRU in the RNN expert with a more powerful bi-directional GRU (Bi-GRU) to better capture sequence context. All other\
    \ hyperparameters and the 50/50 averaging mechanism remain identical to the SOTA baseline for a direct comparison.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n  from axiom.base_architectures import DSConvFeatureExtractor, lse_pool\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 256\n      }\n      \n  class BiDirectionalGRU(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, sequence):\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(self.hidden_dim)\n          \n          # Forward pass\n          forward_scanner = nn.scan(\n              nn.RNN, variable_broadcast='params',\n              split_rngs={'params':\
    \ False}, in_axes=1, out_axes=1\n          )\n          forward_carry, forward_ys = forward_scanner(gru_cell, sequence)\n          \n          # Backward pass\n          reversed_sequence = jnp.flip(sequence, axis=1)\n          backward_scanner = nn.scan(\n              nn.RNN, variable_broadcast='params',\n              split_rngs={'params': False}, in_axes=1, out_axes=1\n          )\n          backward_carry, backward_ys = backward_scanner(gru_cell, reversed_sequence)\n          backward_ys = jnp.flip(backward_ys, axis=1)\n          \n          # Concatenate forward and backward outputs\n          return jnp.concatenate([forward_ys, backward_ys], axis=-1)\n\n  class EnhancedAveragedMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model,\
    \ self.num_blocks, self.dilations, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Expert A: Pooling (Unchanged)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(features_seq, self.tau)\n          pooled_vec = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          # Expert B: Enhanced RNN (Bi-GRU)\n          bi_gru_out = BiDirectionalGRU(self.gru_hidden_dim)(features_seq)\n          # Use the concatenation of the last forward hidden state and the first backward hidden state\n          rnn_vec = jnp.concatenate([bi_gru_out[:, -1, :self.gru_hidden_dim], bi_gru_out[:, 0, self.gru_hidden_dim:]], axis=-1)\n\n          # Project both experts to a common dimension\n          d_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert, name=\"proj_pool\")(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert, name=\"proj_rnn\")(rnn_vec)\n          \n          # Hard-coded 50/50 average\n    \
    \      fused = 0.5 * pooled_vec_proj + 0.5 * rnn_vec_proj\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key); return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = EnhancedAveragedMoE(**{k:v\
    \ for k,v in hparams.items() if k in EnhancedAveragedMoE.__annotations__})\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
