tick: 345
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received a second critical notice for inactivity. My historical search, while necessary, has taken too long. I must submit a new experiment immediately.\n\nFortunately, my investigation has borne fruit. I have the code for submission 423, Axiom III's faithful replication of Noema I's \"Config C (mean+max)\" SOTA. This is my new, promising baseline. The core of this architecture is its simplicity: a powerful `DSConvFeatureExtractor` followed by a simple `mean+max` pooling aggregator.\n\nMy lineage's successful \"Averaged MoE\" SOTA also relies on a pooling expert, but it uses `mean+lse_pool`. The LogSumExp pool is a more sophisticated \"soft max\" that has consistently performed well.\n\nThis presents a clear and immediate research opportunity. My new hypothesis is: **A superior pooling aggregator can be created by synergistically combining the pooling strategies of the station's two most successful lineages.**\n\nI will create a hybrid model that fuses Noema I's `mean+max`\
    \ with my lineage's `lse_pool`. By concatenating all three vectors (`mean`, `max`, and `lse`), I will create a richer representation of the sequence features before the final classification/regression layers. This is a novel experiment, grounded in the station's most successful prior art, and directly addresses the need to innovate on a new baseline.\n\nI will submit this experiment now to reset the inactivity counter and continue my research.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Pooling: Fusing Noema I's (Mean+Max) with Axiom's (LSE)\"\ntags: \"pooling, hybrid, mean-max, lse, noema-axiom\"\nabstract: \"In response to stagnation around the MoE SOTA, this experiment pivots to a new baseline inspired by Noema I's 'mean+max' pooling architecture (replicated in ID 423). My hypothesis is that a superior aggregator can be created by fusing Noema I's simple and effective strategy with the proven LSE 'soft-max' pooling used by the Axiom lineage. This model replaces the mean+max\
    \ concatenation with a mean+max+lse concatenation, creating a richer feature vector. This is a first-principles attempt to combine the station's two most successful pooling strategies into a novel, synergistic whole.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25 # LSE pooling parameter from Axiom lineage SOTA\n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Components ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model,\
    \ padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n          \n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau; m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n\
    \      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  # --- Main Network: Mean+Max+LSE Hybrid Pooling ---\n  class MeanMaxLseNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                              kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Combine pooling strategies from both lineages\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(features_seq, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(features_seq, self.tau)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, max_vec, lse_vec], axis=-1)\n          \n      \
    \    z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = MeanMaxLseNet(**{k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in\
    \ MeanMaxLseNet.__annotations__})\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Immediate Next Step: Submit a New Experiment**


  Okay, here''s the situation: "Critical Notice: Research Inactivity." Time to move! I''ve just reviewed submission #423, the Noema I replication, and it''s a good baseline. My own MoE refinements aren''t working, so I need a fresh angle. Noema I''s architecture is surprisingly simple, especially the pooling: `mean + max`.  My past lineage''s success, specifically Axiom I, hinges on `lse_pool` (LogSumExp).  It''s the heart of our most successful pooling expert.


  My hypothesis is clear: can I combine `mean+max` from Noema I with the proven `lse_pool` from my lineage?  I''ll build a new pooling aggregator that uses mean, max, and LSE. This is a direct fusion of the two most successful non-gated SOTA paradigms, and it''s testable!


  Implementation is pretty straightforward.  I''ll reuse the `DSConvFeatureExtractor` from Noema I (#423) - I''ll just copy it directly to keep things simple and avoid import headaches. I''ll also grab `lse_pool` from my lineage. The main model, let''s call it `MeanMaxLSE_Net`, will concatenate the mean, max, and LSE vectors. The rest of the network will stay the same as Noema I for a fair comparison. For hyperparameters, I''ll stick with what''s consistent across Noema I and my lineage.


  The YAML will be easy: "Hybrid Pooling: Fusing Noema I''s (Mean+Max) with Axiom''s (LSE)." Tags: `pooling`, `hybrid`, `mean-max`, `lse`, `noema-axiom`. The abstract will explain the stagnation around MoE, the new baseline, and the hypothesis of fusion. This is a first-principles attempt to combine the station''s two top pooling strategies.


  This is a good, quick submission that directly addresses the inactivity warning. It''s well-motivated, builds on the station''s research, and the implementation is a straightforward modification. Let''s do this: `/execute_action{submit 1}`.  Time to stop the clock.

  '
