tick: 360
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Multi-Path DSConv\" experiment has failed. This is frustrating, as it was my attempt at a simpler, more reliable innovation.\n\n**Error Analysis:**\nThe error is `TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for -: 'NoneType' and 'DynamicJaxprTracer'`. This error occurred within the `hardcoded_compute_loss` function. It means that `predictions` was `None`. Looking at my code, the `MultiPathDSConvNet`'s `__call__` method has a final line: `return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits`.\n\nCrucially, for tasks that are *not* single-output regression (e.g., `Modif` which is multi-label classification), this line does not have a defined return for all cases. The `if` statement is missing a corresponding `else` to handle other task types. More specifically, the `squeeze` operation is only correct for single-output regression. The other task types (multi-label regression, multi-class classification) should just return `logits`. It seems\
    \ my logic was incomplete. A simple `return logits` at the end would have covered all cases correctly.\n\nThis is a careless but fundamental logic error. The `simple_cpu_validation` passed, which is misleading. It seems the validation only checks for shape mismatches, not for `None` outputs.\n\n**Strategic Conclusion & Final Attempt:**\nI have now failed to implement three novel ideas in a row due to coding errors. My reflection was valuable, but my execution is flawed. My token budget is nearing 50%, and I am still immature. I cannot afford to continue making these kinds of mistakes on complex new architectures.\n\nThe Codex says to be persistent. The station tips say to question fundamentals. I have one last idea that combines these principles. The `DSConv` block works. The `Gumbel-Gated MoE` works. My reflection identified the **fixed dilation pattern** as a weakness. My `Multi-Path` idea was one way to address this. There is a simpler way.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** What if the `DSConv`\
    \ block itself is an expert? The SOTA model uses a `Pooling` expert and an `RNN` expert *after* a shared `DSConv` backbone. What if I create an MoE that chooses between two *different* `DSConv` backbones, each with a different dilation pattern, and feeds the result into a *single*, simple pooling head?\n\nThis is a \"Mixture of Backbones\" approach. It tests my hypothesis about flexible dilations directly, but moves the mixture mechanism to a higher level, reusing the SOTA's gating logic. It is simpler than my multi-path concatenation and less error-prone. This will be my final attempt at a novel architecture before I must consolidate my findings.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mixture of Backbones: Gumbel Gate on Two DSConv Dilation Paths\"\ntags: \"moe, dsconv, backbone, dilation, gating\"\nabstract: \"This is a final attempt to innovate on the SOTA backbone, correcting for previous implementation failures. My reflection suggested the fixed dilation pattern of the\
    \ DSConv block is a key weakness. This experiment tests that insight by creating a Mixture-of-Experts that operates on the backbones themselves. The model uses a Gumbel-Softmax gate to choose between two parallel DSConv backbones, each with a different dilation schedule. The selected feature map is then passed to a single, simple mean+max pooling head. This directly tests if learning to select a dilation pattern on a per-sample basis can outperform the SOTA's fixed backbone.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'gumbel_temp': 0.5\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n\
    \          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i % len(self.dilations)]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def\
    \ gumbel_softmax(logits, temperature, rng_key, deterministic=False):\n      if deterministic:\n          return jax.nn.softmax(logits / temperature)\n      gumbel_noise = -jnp.log(-jnp.log(random.uniform(rng_key, logits.shape) + 1e-20) + 1e-20)\n      y = jax.nn.softmax((logits + gumbel_noise) / temperature)\n      return y\n\n  class BackboneMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dropout_rate: float; gumbel_temp: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Gating decision based on initial projection\n          gating_input = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          gate_logits = nn.Dense(features=2)(gating_input)\n          gumbel_key = self.make_rng('gumbel')\n          gate_weights = gumbel_softmax(gate_logits, self.gumbel_temp, gumbel_key, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Expert Backbone 1: Standard Dilations\n          backbone1 = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks,\
    \ dilations=[1, 2, 4, 8, 16], kernel_size=7, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)\n          features1 = backbone1(x, deterministic)\n\n          # Expert Backbone 2: Wider Dilations\n          backbone2 = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=[1, 3, 9, 27], kernel_size=7, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)\n          features2 = backbone2(x, deterministic)\n          \n          # Mix the backbone outputs\n          features_seq = gate_weights[:, 0, jnp.newaxis, jnp.newaxis] * features1 + \\\n                         gate_weights[:, 1, jnp.newaxis, jnp.newaxis] * features2\n\n          # Use a single, simple pooling head\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(features_seq, axis=1)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, max_vec], axis=-1)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          \n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key, gumbel_key = random.split(rng_key, 3)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key, 'gumbel': gumbel_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          dropout_key, gumbel_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': dropout_key, 'gumbel': gumbel_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = BackboneMoE(**{k: v for k, v in hparams.items()\
    \ if k in BackboneMoE.__annotations__}); return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
